)\u00b2 ] = 2 \u03bc g S (2 M + m )\u00b2\n\nThis is complicated, but if we take m << M, approximate:\n\nM + m \u2248 M\n\n2 M + m \u2248 2 M\n\nm\u00b2 + 4 (M + m )\u00b2 \u2248 4 M\u00b2\n\nThus left-hand side:\n\n[ (m\u00b2 v\u00b2 ) / M\u00b2 - 2 \u03bc g S ] * 4 M\u00b2 \u2248 [ (m\u00b2 v\u00b2 / M\u00b2 ) - 2 \u03bc g S ] * 4 M\u00b2\n\nRight-hand side:\n\n2 \u03bc g S (4 M\u00b2 ) = 8 \u03bc g S M\u00b2\n\nTherefore, equate:\n\n[ (m\u00b2 v\u00b2 / M\u00b2 ) - 2 \u03bc g S ] * 4 M\u00b2 \u2248 8 \u03bc g S M\u00b2\n\nDivide both sides by 4 M\u00b2:\n\n( m\u00b2 v\u00b2 / M\u00b2 - 2 \u03bc g S ) \u2248 2 \u03bc g S\n\nThus:\n\nm\u00b2 v\u00b2 / M\u00b2 \u2248 4 \u03bc g S\n\nMultiply both sides by M\u00b2 / m\u00b2:\n\nv\u00b2 \u2248 (4 \u03bc g S M\u00b2 ) / m\u00b2\n\nTake square root:\n\nv \u2248 (2 M / m ) sqrt( \u03bc g S )\n\nWhich matches our approximate result.\n\nTherefore, despite the complexity of the exact equations, the approximation due to m << M gives us v \u2248 (2 M / m ) sqrt( \u03bc g S )\n\nTherefore, the required bullet speed is (2 M / m ) sqrt( \u03bc g S )\n\nLet me check dimensions to ensure:\n\nM is mass, m is mass, \u03bc is dimensionless, g is acceleration, S is distance.\n\nsqrt( \u03bc g S ) has dimensions sqrt( (L T^{-2}) L ) = sqrt( L\u00b2 T^{-2} ) = L T^{-1}, which is velocity. Then, (M / m ) is dimensionless (since M and m are masses). So overall, (M/m ) * velocity gives velocity. So the dimensions check out.\n\nTherefore, the answer should be v = (2 M / m ) sqrt( \u03bc g S )", "deepseek_solution": "To determine the required speed \\( v \\) of the bullet for the final distance between the blocks to remain \\( S \\), we analyze the problem in stages:\n\n1. **Collision of Bullet and Left Block**:  \n   Using conservation of momentum for the inelastic collision:  \n   \\[\n   m v = (M + m) u \\implies u = \\frac{m}{M + m} v \\approx \\frac{m}{M} v \\quad (\\text{since } m \\ll M).\n   \\]\n\n2. **Sliding to Collision**:  \n   The left block slides distance \\( S \\) under friction. The velocity just before collision \\( u' \\) is derived from kinematic equations:  \n   \\[\n   u' = \\sqrt{u^2 - 2 \\mu g S}.\n   \\]\n\n3. **Elastic Collision Between Blocks**:  \n   Using conservation of momentum and kinetic energy (elastic collision), and approximating \\( M + m \\approx M \\), the post-collision velocities are:  \n   \\[\n   v_1 \\approx \\frac{m}{2M} u', \\quad v_2 \\approx u'.\n   \\]\n\n4. **Post-Collision Sliding**:  \n   The distances traveled by each block until stopping are:  \n   \\[\n   d_1 = \\frac{v_1^2}{2 \\mu g}, \\quad d_2 = \\frac{v_2^2}{2 \\mu g}.\n   \\]\n   The total distance after stopping must equal \\( S \\):  \n   \\[\n   \\frac{v_1^2 + v_2^2}{2 \\mu g} = S \\implies v_1^2 + v_2^2 = 2 \\mu g S.\n   \\]\n\n5. **Substitute Velocities and Solve**:  \n   Using \\( v_1 \\approx \\frac{m}{2M} u' \\) and \\( v_2 \\approx u' \\), substitute into the distance equation:  \n   \\[\n   \\left( \\frac{m^2}{4M^2} u'^2 \\right) + u'^2 \\approx 2 \\mu g S \\implies u'^2 \\approx 2 \\mu g S.\n   \\]\n   Substitute \\( u' = \\sqrt{u^2 - 2 \\mu g S} \\):  \n   \\[\n   u^2 - 2 \\mu g S = 2 \\mu g S \\implies u^2 = 4 \\mu g S \\implies u = 2 \\sqrt{\\mu g S}.\n   \\]\n   Finally, relate \\( u \\) to \\( v \\):  \n   \\[\n   \\frac{m}{M} v = 2 \\sqrt{\\mu g S} \\implies v = \\frac{2M}{m} \\sqrt{\\mu g S}.\n   \\]\n\n**Final Answer**:  \n\\[\n\\boxed{v = \\dfrac{2M}{m} \\sqrt{\\mu g S}}\n\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "\n1. **Initial Setup and Assumptions**:\n    - Two identical blocks of mass \\(M\\) each are placed on a horizontal surface with a distance \\(S\\) between them.\n    - A bullet of mass \\(m\\) is fired horizontally and embeds itself into the left block.\n    - The collision between the blocks is perfectly elastic (absolute elastic collision).\n    - The mass of the bullet \\(m\\) is much smaller than the mass of the block \\(M\\) (\\(m \\ll M\\)).\n    - The coefficient of friction between the blocks and the horizontal surface is \\(\\mu\\).\n    - The acceleration due to gravity is \\(g\\).\n\n2. **Conservation of Momentum**:\n    Use the conservation of momentum to find the velocity of the left block immediately after the bullet embeds itself. \n\n    By the law of conservation of momentum:\n\n    \\[\n    m v = (M + m) u \\approx M u \\quad \\text{(since \\(m \\ll M\\))}\n    \\]\n\n    Solving for \\(u\\):\n\n    \\[\n    u = \\frac{m v}{M}\n    \\]\n\n3. **Perfectly Elastic Collision**:\n    In a perfectly elastic collision between two identical masses, they exchange velocities. Thus, after the collision:\n    - The left block, which initially had velocity \\(u\\), will come to rest.\n    - The right block, which was initially at rest, will move with velocity \\(u\\).\n\n4. **Kinetic Energy and Work Done Against Friction**:\n    Applying the work-energy theorem to the subsequent motion of the blocks:\n\n    The kinetic energy of the moving block (\\(M\\)) immediately after the collision is given by:\n\n    \\[\n    \\frac{1}{2} M u^2\n    \\]\n\n    The work done against friction \\(A_{\\text{tr}}\\) when the block moves distance \\(S\\) and comes to a stop is:\n\n    \\[\n    A_{\\text{tr}} = \\mu M g S\n    \\]\n\n    In the final state, since the blocks should come to rest when they are again distance \\(S\\) apart, equate the kinetic energy to the work done:\n\n    \\[\n    \\frac{1}{2} M u^2 = 2 \\mu M g S\n    \\]\n\n5. **Solving for the Final Velocity**:\n    Simplify the above equation to solve for \\(u\\):\n\n    \\[\n    \\frac{1}{2} u^2 = 2 \\mu g S\n    \\]\n\n    \\[\n    u^2 = 4 \\mu g S\n    \\]\n\n    \\[\n    u = \\sqrt{4 \\mu g S}\n    \\]\n\n6. **Substituting \\(u\\) Back to Find \\(v\\)**:\n    Substitute \\(u\\) back into the equation found from the conservation of momentum:\n\n    \\[\n    u = \\frac{m v}{M} = \\sqrt{4 \\mu g S}\n    \\]\n\n    Therefore:\n\n    \\[\n    v = \\frac{M}{m} \\sqrt{4 \\mu g S} = \\frac{2 M}{m} \\sqrt{\\mu g S}\n    \\]\n\n### Conclusion:\nThe required speed of the bullet is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{v = \\frac{2 M}{m} \\sqrt{\\mu g S}}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "It is known that \\(\\frac{1}{3a} + \\frac{2}{3b} = \\frac{3}{a + 2b}\\). Prove that \\(a = b\\).", "deepseek_reasoning": "Alright, so I need to prove that a equals b given the equation (1/(3a)) + (2/(3b)) = 3/(a + 2b). Let me start by understanding what this equation is saying and how I can manipulate it to show that a and b must be equal.\n\nFirst, maybe I should write down the equation again to have it clear:\n\n(1)/(3a) + (2)/(3b) = 3/(a + 2b)\n\nHmm, okay. Both sides have denominators involving a and b. Maybe I can simplify this by getting rid of the fractions. To do that, I can find a common denominator or cross-multiply. Let's see. Let's try cross-multiplying to eliminate the denominators. But first, maybe combine the left side into a single fraction.\n\nOn the left side, the two terms are (1)/(3a) and (2)/(3b). Since they have denominators 3a and 3b, the common denominator would be 3ab. Let me combine them:\n\n[(1 * b) + (2 * a)] / (3ab) = 3/(a + 2b)\n\nSo, the left side becomes (b + 2a)/(3ab). So the equation is:\n\n(b + 2a)/(3ab) = 3/(a + 2b)\n\nNow, let's cross-multiply to eliminate the fractions. Multiply both sides by 3ab(a + 2b):\n\n(b + 2a)(a + 2b) = 9ab\n\nOkay, expanding the left side. Let's do that step by step.\n\nFirst, multiply (b + 2a)(a + 2b). Use the distributive property (FOIL):\n\n= b*a + b*2b + 2a*a + 2a*2b\n\nCalculate each term:\n\n= ab + 2b\u00b2 + 2a\u00b2 + 4ab\n\nCombine like terms:\n\nab + 4ab = 5ab\n\nSo the left side becomes 2a\u00b2 + 5ab + 2b\u00b2\n\nWait, hold on:\n\nWait, let me check the multiplication again. Maybe I made a mistake there.\n\nFirst term: b * a = ab\n\nSecond term: b * 2b = 2b\u00b2\n\nThird term: 2a * a = 2a\u00b2\n\nFourth term: 2a * 2b = 4ab\n\nSo adding those up: ab + 2b\u00b2 + 2a\u00b2 + 4ab\n\nCombine ab and 4ab: 5ab\n\nSo total is 2a\u00b2 + 5ab + 2b\u00b2\n\nYes, that's correct. So left side is 2a\u00b2 + 5ab + 2b\u00b2\n\nRight side is 9ab\n\nSo the equation becomes:\n\n2a\u00b2 + 5ab + 2b\u00b2 = 9ab\n\nSubtract 9ab from both sides to bring everything to the left:\n\n2a\u00b2 + 5ab + 2b\u00b2 - 9ab = 0\n\nSimplify the middle terms:\n\n5ab - 9ab = -4ab\n\nSo equation is:\n\n2a\u00b2 - 4ab + 2b\u00b2 = 0\n\nHmm, this looks like a quadratic in terms of a and b. Let me factor this equation.\n\nFirst, note that all coefficients are even, so factor out a 2:\n\n2(a\u00b2 - 2ab + b\u00b2) = 0\n\nDivide both sides by 2:\n\na\u00b2 - 2ab + b\u00b2 = 0\n\nWait a second, that's a perfect square:\n\n(a - b)\u00b2 = 0\n\nBecause (a - b)\u00b2 = a\u00b2 - 2ab + b\u00b2\n\nTherefore, (a - b)\u00b2 = 0\n\nTaking the square root of both sides:\n\na - b = 0 => a = b\n\nSo that's the proof. Therefore, a must equal b.\n\nBut wait, let me check my steps again to make sure I didn't make a mistake.\n\nStarting from the original equation:\n\n1/(3a) + 2/(3b) = 3/(a + 2b)\n\nCombined left side:\n\n(b + 2a)/(3ab) = 3/(a + 2b)\n\nCross-multiplied:\n\n(b + 2a)(a + 2b) = 9ab\n\nExpanded left side:\n\nab + 2b\u00b2 + 2a\u00b2 + 4ab = 2a\u00b2 + 5ab + 2b\u00b2\n\nSet equal to 9ab:\n\n2a\u00b2 + 5ab + 2b\u00b2 = 9ab\n\nSubtract 9ab:\n\n2a\u00b2 - 4ab + 2b\u00b2 = 0\n\nFactor out 2:\n\n2(a\u00b2 - 2ab + b\u00b2) = 0 => 2(a - b)^2 = 0\n\nTherefore, (a - b)^2 = 0 => a = b\n\nLooks correct. So the conclusion is a = b. Therefore, proven.\n\nBut wait, just to be thorough, maybe check with some numbers. Suppose a = b, let's pick a = b = 1.\n\nLeft side: 1/(3*1) + 2/(3*1) = 1/3 + 2/3 = 1\n\nRight side: 3/(1 + 2*1) = 3/3 = 1\n\nSo equality holds. If a = b = 2:\n\nLeft side: 1/(6) + 2/(6) = 1/6 + 1/3 = 1/6 + 2/6 = 3/6 = 1/2\n\nRight side: 3/(2 + 4) = 3/6 = 1/2. Correct.\n\nNow, test with a \u2260 b. Suppose a = 2, b = 1. Then:\n\nLeft side: 1/(6) + 2/(3*1) = 1/6 + 2/3 = 1/6 + 4/6 = 5/6\n\nRight side: 3/(2 + 2*1) = 3/4 = 0.75\n\nBut 5/6 \u2248 0.833..., which is not equal to 0.75. So equality doesn't hold here. Which supports that a must equal b.\n\nAnother test: a = 3, b = 3.\n\nLeft: 1/9 + 2/9 = 3/9 = 1/3\n\nRight: 3/(3 + 6) = 3/9 = 1/3. Correct.\n\nIf a = 1, b = 2:\n\nLeft: 1/3 + 2/(6) = 1/3 + 1/3 = 2/3\n\nRight: 3/(1 + 4) = 3/5 = 0.6 \u2260 2/3 \u2248 0.666...\n\nSo again, not equal. So seems like only when a = b does the equation hold. Therefore, the proof is valid.\n\nTherefore, the conclusion is a = b.", "deepseek_solution": "To prove that \\( a = b \\) given the equation \\( \\frac{1}{3a} + \\frac{2}{3b} = \\frac{3}{a + 2b} \\), follow these steps:\n\n1. **Combine the left-hand side fractions:**\n   \\[\n   \\frac{1}{3a} + \\frac{2}{3b} = \\frac{b + 2a}{3ab}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Set the combined fraction equal to the right-hand side:**\n   \\[\n   \\frac{b + 2a}{3ab} = \\frac{3}{a + 2b}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Cross-multiply to eliminate denominators:**\n   \\[\n   (b + 2a)(a + 2b) = 9ab\n   \\]\n\n4. **Expand the left-hand side:**\n   \\[\n   ab + 2b^2 + 2a^2 + 4ab = 2a^2 + 5ab + 2b^2\n   \\]\n\n5. **Equate and simplify:**\n   \\[\n   2a^2 + 5ab + 2b^2 = 9ab \\implies 2a^2 - 4ab + 2b^2 = 0\n   \\]\n\n6. **Factor the equation:**\n   \\[\n   2(a^2 - 2ab + b^2) = 0 \\implies 2(a - b)^2 = 0\n   \\]\n\n7. **Conclude from the factored form:**\n   \\[\n   (a - b)^2 = 0 \\implies a = b\n   \\]\n\n**Final Answer:** \\( \\boxed{a = b} \\)", "ground_truth_solution": "\n**1-\u0439 \u0441\u043f\u043e\u0441\u043e\u0431:**\n\n1. \u0423\u043c\u043d\u043e\u0436\u0438\u043c \u043e\u0431\u0435 \u0447\u0430\u0441\u0442\u0438 \u0438\u0441\u0445\u043e\u0434\u043d\u043e\u0433\u043e \u0443\u0440\u0430\u0432\u043d\u0435\u043d\u0438\u044f \u043d\u0430 \\(3ab(a + 2b)\\):\n   \\[\n   \\frac{1}{3a} + \\frac{2}{3b} = \\frac{3}{a + 2b}\n   \\]\n   \u0441\u0442\u0430\u043d\u043e\u0432\u0438\u0442\u0441\u044f\n   \\[\n   3ab(a + 2b) \\left(\\frac{1}{3a} + \\frac{2}{3b}\\right) = 3ab(a + 2b) \\left(\\frac{3}{a + 2b}\\right).\n   \\]\n   \n2. \u0423\u043f\u0440\u043e\u0441\u0442\u0438\u043c \u043b\u0435\u0432\u0443\u044e \u0438 \u043f\u0440\u0430\u0432\u0443\u044e \u0447\u0430\u0441\u0442\u0438 \u0443\u0440\u0430\u0432\u043d\u0435\u043d\u0438\u044f:\n   \\[\n   b(a + 2b) + 2a(a + 2b) = 9ab.\n   \\]\n   \n3. \u0420\u0430\u0441\u043a\u0440\u043e\u0435\u043c \u0441\u043a\u043e\u0431\u043a\u0438:\n   \\[\n   ab + 2b^2 + 2a^2 + 4ab = 9ab.\n   \\]\n   \n4. \u0421\u043b\u043e\u0436\u0438\u043c \u043f\u043e\u0434\u043e\u0431\u043d\u044b\u0435 \u0447\u043b\u0435\u043d\u044b \u0438 \u0443\u043f\u0440\u043e\u0441\u0442\u0438\u043c:\n   \\[\n   ab + 4ab + 2b^2 + 2a^2 = 9ab \\implies 5ab + 2b^2 + 2a^2 = 9ab \\implies 2a^2 + 2b^2 + 5ab - 9ab = 0.\n   \\]\n   \n5. \u0423\u043f\u0440\u043e\u0441\u0442\u0438\u043c \u043f\u043e\u043b\u0443\u0447\u0435\u043d\u043d\u043e\u0435 \u0443\u0440\u0430\u0432\u043d\u0435\u043d\u0438\u0435 \u0435\u0449\u0451 \u0440\u0430\u0437:\n   \\[\n   2a^2 + 2b^2 - 4ab = 0 \\implies 2a^2 + 2b^2 - 4ab = 0.\n   \\]\n   \n6. \u0412\u044b\u043d\u0435\u0441\u0435\u043c \u043e\u0431\u0449\u0438\u0439 \u043c\u043d\u043e\u0436\u0438\u0442\u0435\u043b\u044c:\n   \\[\n   2(a^2 + b^2 - 2ab) = 0 \\implies a^2 + b^2 - 2ab = 0.\n   \\]\n   \n7. \u0412\u043e\u0441\u043f\u043e\u043b\u044c\u0437\u0443\u0435\u043c\u0441\u044f \u0444\u043e\u0440\u043c\u0443\u043b\u043e\u0439 \u043a\u0432\u0430\u0434\u0440\u0430\u0442\u0430 \u0440\u0430\u0437\u043d\u043e\u0441\u0442\u0438:\n   \\[\n   (a - b)^2 = 0.\n   \\]\n   \n8. \u0418\u0437 \u043f\u043e\u043b\u0443\u0447\u0435\u043d\u043d\u043e\u0433\u043e \u0443\u0440\u0430\u0432\u043d\u0435\u043d\u0438\u044f \u0441\u043b\u0435\u0434\u0443\u0435\u0442:\n   \\[\n   a - b = 0 \\implies a = b.\n   \\]\n\n**2-\u0439 \u0441\u043f\u043e\u0441\u043e\u0431:**\n\n1. \u041f\u0435\u0440\u0435\u0432\u0435\u0434\u0451\u043c \u043b\u0435\u0432\u0443\u044e \u0447\u0430\u0441\u0442\u044c \u0443\u