ss than 40.416.\n\nThe point (-\u221a(5/2), \u221a(5/2)): f(x, y) \u2248 17.514, which is the lowest.\n\nIs there any other point on the disk where f(x, y) could be higher or lower?\n\nSuppose we take a point in the interior. However, since f is linear in each region, the maximum/minimum can't be in the interior because the gradient is non-zero. Therefore, the extrema must be on the boundary.\n\nHence, I think we've covered all possibilities.\n\nTherefore, the final answer should be:\n\nMaximum value: 6\u221a5 + 27\n\nMinimum value: -3\u221a10 + 27\n\nBut let me present them in the boxed format as required.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe maximum value is \\(\\boxed{27 + 6\\sqrt{5}}\\) and the minimum value is \\(\\boxed{27 - 3\\sqrt{10}}\\).", "deepseek_solution": "Given real numbers \\( x \\) and \\( y \\) that satisfy \\( x^2 + y^2 \\leq 5 \\), we need to find the maximum and minimum values of the function \\( f(x, y) = 3|x+y| + |4y+9| + |7y-3x-18| \\).\n\nFirst, we simplify the function by analyzing the absolute value expressions within the constraint \\( x^2 + y^2 \\leq 5 \\):\n\n1. The term \\( |4y + 9| \\) is always positive within the disk because \\( y \\geq -\\sqrt{5} \\approx -2.236 \\), and \\( 4(-\\sqrt{5}) + 9 \\approx 0.056 > 0 \\). Thus, \\( |4y + 9| = 4y + 9 \\).\n2. The term \\( |7y - 3x - 18| \\) is always negative within the disk because substituting \\( (0, 0) \\) gives \\( -18 \\), and the line \\( 7y - 3x - 18 = 0 \\) does not intersect the disk. Thus, \\( |7y - 3x - 18| = -7y + 3x + 18 \\).\n\nThis simplifies the function to:\n\\[ f(x, y) = 3|x + y| + 4y + 9 - 7y + 3x + 18 = 3|x + y| + 3x - 3y + 27 \\]\n\nWe consider two cases based on the sign of \\( x + y \\):\n\n**Case 1: \\( x + y \\geq 0 \\)**\n- Here, \\( |x + y| = x + y \\), so \\( f(x, y) = 3(x + y) + 3x - 3y + 27 = 6x + 27 \\).\n- The maximum value occurs at \\( (x, y) = (\\sqrt{5}, 0) \\) giving \\( 6\\sqrt{5} + 27 \\).\n- The minimum value occurs at \\( (x, y) = (-\\sqrt{5/2}, \\sqrt{5/2}) \\) giving \\( -3\\sqrt{10} + 27 \\).\n\n**Case 2: \\( x + y \\leq 0 \\)**\n- Here, \\( |x + y| = -(x + y) \\), so \\( f(x, y) = -3(x + y) + 3x - 3y + 27 = -6y + 27 \\).\n- The maximum value occurs at \\( (x, y) = (0, -\\sqrt{5}) \\) giving \\( 6\\sqrt{5} + 27 \\).\n- The minimum value occurs at \\( (x, y) = (\\sqrt{5/2}, -\\sqrt{5/2}) \\) giving \\( -3\\sqrt{10} + 27 \\).\n\nThe points \\( (\\sqrt{5}, 0) \\) and \\( (0, -\\sqrt{5}) \\) give the maximum value, while the points \\( (-\\sqrt{5/2}, \\sqrt{5/2}) \\) and \\( (\\sqrt{5/2}, -\\sqrt{5/2}) \\) give the minimum value.\n\nThus, the maximum value of \\( f(x, y) \\) is \\(\\boxed{27 + 6\\sqrt{5}}\\) and the minimum value is \\(\\boxed{27 - 3\\sqrt{10}}\\).", "ground_truth_solution": "\nGiven the function \\( f(x, y) = 3 |x+y| + |4y + 9| + |7y - 3x - 18| \\) under the constraint \\( x^2 + y^2 \\leqslant 5 \\),\n\n1. **Expressing in Polar Coordinates:**\n\n   Let \\( x = r \\cos \\theta \\) and \\( y = r \\sin \\theta \\), where \\( r \\geq 0 \\) and \\( 0 \\leq \\theta < 2\\pi \\). This gives us:\n   \\[\n   0 \\leq r \\leq \\sqrt{5}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Considering the terms separately, we note:**\n   \\[\n   4y + 9 = 4r \\sin \\theta + 9\n   \\]\n   Since \\( -\\sqrt{5} \\leqslant y \\leqslant \\sqrt{5} \\),\n   \\[\n   4y \\geq -4 \\sqrt{5} \\implies 4r \\sin \\theta \\geq -4\\sqrt{5} \\implies 4r \\sin \\theta + 9 > 0\n   \\]\n   Therefore,\n   \\[\n   |4y + 9| = 4r \\sin \\theta + 9\n   \\]\n\n3. **Simplifying further using the given constraints:**\n\n   Consider the term \\( 7y - 3x - 18 = 7r \\sin \\theta - 3r \\cos \\theta - 18 \\):\n\n   This can be rewritten using the trigonometric identity as:\n   \\[\n   7r \\sin \\theta - 3r \\cos \\theta = r \\cdot \\sqrt{58} \\sin (\\theta - \\varphi)\n   \\]\n   where \\( \\varphi = \\tan^{-1} \\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right) \\).\n\n   Since \\( 0 \\leq r \\leq \\sqrt{5} \\),\n   \\[\n   7r \\sin \\theta - 3r \\cos \\theta \\leq \\sqrt{58} r\n   \\]\n   Therefore,\n   \\[\n   r \\sqrt{58} \\sin (\\theta - \\varphi) - 18 \\leq \\sqrt{5} \\sqrt{58} - 18 < 0\n   \\]\n   This implies:\n   \\[\n   |7y - 3x - 18| = -7r \\sin \\theta + 3r \\cos \\theta + 18\n   \\]\n\n   Simplifying the function:\n   \\[\n   f(x, y) = 3|x + y| + 4y + 9 - 7y + 3x + 18\n   \\]\n\n4. **Evaluating \\(f(x, y)\\) for the two principal cases:**\n\n   - **Case (i): \\( \\sin \\left( \\theta + \\frac{\\pi}{4} \\right) \\geq 0 \\)**\n\n     This occurs when \\( 0 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\frac{3\\pi}{4} \\) or \\( \\frac{7\\pi}{4} \\leq \\theta < 2\\pi \\). Therefore,\n     \\[\n     f(x, y) = 3 \\sqrt{2} r \\sin \\left( \\theta + \\frac{\\pi}{4} \\right) + 3 \\sqrt{2} r \\cos \\left( \\theta + \\frac{\\pi}{4} \\right) + 27\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     = 6 r \\cos \\theta + 27\n     \\]\n\n     Finding maximum and minimum values:\n     - When \\( \\cos \\theta = 1 \\): \\( f_{\\text{max}} = 6\\sqrt{5} + 27 \\)\n     - When \\( \\cos \\theta = -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\): \\( f_{\\text{min}} = 27 - 3\\sqrt{10} \\)\n\n   - **Case (ii): \\( \\sin \\left( \\theta + \\frac{\\pi}{4} \\right) \\leq 0 \\)**\n\n     This occurs when \\( \\frac{3\\pi}{4} \\leq \\theta \\leq \\frac{7\\pi}{4} \\). Therefore,\n     \\[\n     f(x, y) = -3 \\sqrt{2} r \\sin \\left( \\theta + \\frac{\\pi}{4} \\right) + 3 \\sqrt{2} r \\cos \\left( \\theta + \\frac{\\pi}{4} \\right) + 27\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     = -6r \\sin \\theta + 27\n     \\]\n\n     Finding maximum and minimum values:\n     - When \\( \\sin \\theta = -1 \\): \\( f_{\\text{max}} = 27 + 6\\sqrt{5} \\)\n     - When \\( \\sin \\theta = \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\): \\( f_{\\text{min}} = 27 - 3\\sqrt{10} \\)\n\n### Conclusion:\n\nCombining both cases, we get:\n\\[\nf(x, y)_{\\text{max}} = 27 + 6\\sqrt{5}, \\quad f(x, y)_{\\text{min}} = 27 - 3\\sqrt{10}\n\\]\n\n\\(\\boxed{27 + 6 \\sqrt{5}}\\)", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "$M$ is a subset of $\\{1,2,3, \\ldots, 15\\}$ such tha