nute, then 0.6 * 30 is 18. All methods converge. Therefore, the answer is D. \n\nI think that's solid. All different approaches confirm the same answer. So, I don't see any issues here. Therefore, the correct answer should be D) 18 km.", "deepseek_solution": "Daniel's cycling speed is constant. To determine how far he cycled in 30 minutes:\n\n1. **Convert time to hours**:  \n   - 40 minutes = \\(\\frac{40}{60} = \\frac{2}{3}\\) hours.  \n   - 30 minutes = \\(\\frac{30}{60} = 0.5\\) hours.  \n\n2. **Calculate speed**:  \n   \\[\n   \\text{Speed} = \\frac{\\text{Distance}}{\\text{Time}} = \\frac{24 \\, \\text{km}}{\\frac{2}{3} \\, \\text{hours}} = 24 \\times \\frac{3}{2} = 36 \\, \\text{km/h}.\n   \\]\n\n3. **Distance for 30 minutes**:  \n   \\[\n   \\text{Distance} = \\text{Speed} \\times \\text{Time} = 36 \\, \\text{km/h} \\times 0.5 \\, \\text{hours} = 18 \\, \\text{km}.\n   \\]\n\n**Verification**:  \n- **Proportional reasoning**:  \n  \\(\\frac{30}{40} \\times 24 \\, \\text{km} = \\frac{3}{4} \\times 24 = 18 \\, \\text{km}\\).  \n- **Per-minute rate**:  \n  \\(\\frac{24 \\, \\text{km}}{40 \\, \\text{min}} = 0.6 \\, \\text{km/min} \\implies 0.6 \\times 30 = 18 \\, \\text{km}\\).  \n\nAll methods confirm the distance is **18 km**.\n\n**Answer**: \\(\\boxed{D}\\)", "ground_truth_solution": "\n1. **Determine the speed Daniel is riding:**\n\n   Daniel cycled $24 \\, \\text{km}$ in $40 \\, \\text{minutes}$. First, we convert 40 minutes into hours since speed is typically measured in $\\text{km/h}$. \n\n   \\[\n   40 \\, \\text{minutes} = \\frac{40}{60} \\, \\text{hours} = \\frac{2}{3} \\, \\text{hours}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Calculate Daniel's speed in km/h:**\n\n   Now, we use the distance and time to find the speed \\( v \\):\n\n   \\[\n   v = \\frac{\\text{distance}}{\\text{time}} = \\frac{24 \\, \\text{km}}{\\frac{2}{3} \\, \\text{hours}} = 24 \\, \\text{km} \\times \\frac{3}{2} = 36 \\, \\text{km/h}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Find the distance cycled in 30 minutes:**\n\n   To find 