oot gives 3, 6, 9, etc., which are multiples of 3. So, if the sequence is in multiples of 3, it can hit a square (which must be a multiple of 9), then drop down to a smaller multiple of 3 (like 3, 6, 9), and then cycle.\n\nTherefore, starting from a multiple of 3, the sequence stays in multiples of 3. If it eventually reaches a multiple of 9, then it drops to a lower multiple of 3, and if that lower multiple leads back into the cycle 3 \u2192 6 \u2192 9 \u2192 3..., then it's valid.\n\nBut wait, if you start at 3: 3 \u2192 6 \u2192 9 \u2192 3..., which is a cycle.\n\nIf you start at 6: 6 \u2192 9 \u2192 3 \u2192 6..., same cycle.\n\nIf you start at 9: 9 \u2192 3 \u2192 6 \u2192 9..., same cycle.\n\nIf you start at 12: 12 \u2192 15 \u2192 18 \u2192 21 \u2192 24 \u2192 27 \u2192 30 \u2192 33 \u2192 36 \u2192 6 \u2192 9 \u2192 3..., enters the cycle.\n\nSimilarly, starting at 15: 15 \u2192 18 \u2192 21 \u2192 24 \u2192 27 \u2192 30 \u2192 33 \u2192 36 \u2192 6..., same.\n\nTherefore, starting from any multiple of 3, the sequence will keep adding 3 until it reaches a multiple of 9, which is a square. Then, taking the square root gives a multiple of 3 (since sqrt(9k\u00b2) = 3k). For example, 36 is 6\u00b2, sqrt(36) = 6. Then 6 + 3 = 9, sqrt(9) = 3, 3 + 3 = 6, and so on. So, the cycle is 3, 6, 9.\n\nBut wait, what if the multiple of 3 is larger, say 3k where k is not a multiple of 3. For example, 3*5 = 15. Then, adding 3s until we reach a multiple of 9. 15, 18, 21, 24, 27 (which is 9*3). So sqrt(27) is not integer, so we add 3 to get 30, then 33, 36. 36 is 6\u00b2, sqrt(36) = 6. Then proceed as before.\n\nTherefore, any multiple of 3 will eventually reach 36 (or some higher multiple of 9), then reduce to 6, then cycle. Therefore, all multiples of 3 are valid starting points.\n\nNow, let's check numbers that are 1 mod 3. For example, 7: 1 mod 3. As before, 7 \u2192 10 \u2192 13 \u2192 16. 16 is a square (4\u00b2), so sqrt(16) = 4. Then 4 is 1 mod 3. sqrt(4) = 2, which is 