ince we suspect that all such lines pass through (-1, 2), let's verify that point (-1, 2) lies on line AB for any t.\n\nSo, if we substitute x = -1, y = 2 into the equation of line AB, it should hold true for any t.\n\nFirst, let's parametrize the line AB. The parametric equations can be written as:\n\nx = t + s*( x_B - x_A )\n\ny = t\u00b2 + s*( y_B - y_A )\n\nBut maybe it's easier to use the point-slope form. Alternatively, let's check if (-1, 2) satisfies the equation of line AB.\n\nAlternatively, compute the equation of line AB using points A(t, t\u00b2) and B(b, b\u00b2), and check if (-1, 2) satisfies it.\n\nThe equation of line AB can be written as:\n\n(y - t\u00b2) = m(x - t)\n\nWhere m is the slope computed as (b\u00b2 - t\u00b2)/(b - t)\n\nSo, let's compute m:\n\nm = (b\u00b2 - t\u00b2)/(b - t) = (b - t)(b + t)/(b - t) ) = b + t, provided that b \u2260 t.\n\nSince for right-angled triangles, points A and B are distinct, so b \u2260 t.\n\nTherefore, m = b + t.\n\nGiven that from previous, b = - (t + 2)/(t + 1)\n\nThus, m = [ - (t + 2)/(t + 1) + t ] = [ - (t + 2) + t(t + 1) ] / (t + 1 )\n\n= [ -t - 2 + t\u00b2 + t ] / (t + 1 )\n\n= (t\u00b2 - 2)/(t + 1 )\n\nTherefore, slope m = (t\u00b2 - 2)/(t + 1 )\n\nTherefore, equation of line AB is:\n\ny - t\u00b2 = [ (t\u00b2 - 2)/(t + 1 ) ] (x - t )\n\nNow, let's check if (-1, 2) satisfies this equation.\n\nLeft side: 2 - t\u00b2\n\nRight side: [ (t\u00b2 - 2)/(t + 1 ) ] ( -1 - t )\n\nSimplify right side:\n\n[ (t\u00b2 - 2)/(t + 1 ) ] * ( - (1 + t ) ) = - (t\u00b2 - 2 )\n\nTherefore, equation:\n\n2 - t\u00b2 = - (t\u00b2 - 2 )\n\nLeft side: 2 - t\u00b2\n\nRight side: -t\u00b2 + 2 = 2 - t\u00b2\n\nHence, both sides are equal. Therefore, (-1, 2) lies on every such line AB. Therefore, regardless of the value of t, the line AB passes through (-1, 2). Therefore, all such hypotenuses AB pass through the point (-1, 2). Similarly, the same applies to CD, which is another hypotenuse of a different triangle, so CD also passes t