 \\]\n\n2. **Intercepts Calculation:**\n\n    Let\u2019s calculate the intercepts $a_n$ (x-intercept) and $b_n$ (y-intercept) for the line $l_{n}$.\n    \n    - The x-intercept is found by setting $y = 0$ in the line equation:\n    \n        \\[\n        0 = k_n(x - 1) + 1\n        \\]\n        \\[\n        -1 = k_n(x - 1)\n        \\]\n        \\[\n        x = 1 - \\frac{1}{k_n}\n        \\]\n        \n        Therefore, \n        \\[\n        a_n = 1 - \\frac{1}{k_n}\n        \\]\n\n    - The y-intercept is found by setting $x = 0$ in the line equation:\n\n        \\[\n        y = k_n(0 - 1) + 1 = -k_n + 1\n        \\]\n\n        Therefore, \n        \\[\n        b_n = 1 - k_n\n        \\]\n\n3. **Forming $k_{n+1}$:**\n\n    Given condition (II):\n    \\[\n    k_{n+1} = a_n - b_n\n    \\]\n    \n    Substitute $a_n$ and $b_n$ into this equation:\n    \n    \\[\n    k_{n+1} = \\left(1 - \\frac{1}{k_n}\\right) - (1 - k_n)\n    \\]\n    Simplifying this, we get\n    \\[\n    k_{n+1} = 1 - \\frac{1}{k_n} - 1 + k_n\n    \\]\n    \\[\n    k_{n+1} = k_n - \\frac{1}{k_n}\n    \\]\n\n4. **Analyzing signs of slopes:**\n\n    From condition (III), we know:\n    \\[\n    k_n k_{n+1} \\geq 0\n    \\]\n\n    This implies that the slopes $k_n$ and $k_{n+1}$ must have the same sign, either both positive or both negative.\n    Therefore, if $k_n > 0$, then $k_{n+1} > 0$ and if $k_n < 0$, then $k_{n+1} < 0$.\n\n5. **Behavior and Limits:**\n\n    Let's consider the sequence $k_n$:\n\n    - If $k_n > 0$, the sequence $\\{k_n\\}$ is positive decreasing.\n    - If $k_n < 0$, the sequence $\\{k_n\\}$ is negative increasing.\n\n    Both sequences are bounded by 0, thus:\n\n    \\[\n    \\lim_{n \\to \\infty} k_n = k\n    \\]\n\n    Taking the limit of the recursive relationship:\n    \\[\n    k_{n+1} = k_n - \\frac{1}{k_n}\n    \\]\n    as $n \\to \\infty$, we get:\n    \\[\n    k = k - \\frac{1}{k}\n    \\]\n    This simplifies to:\n    \\[\n    -\\frac{1}{k} = 0\n    \\]\n    which is 