ence, overlapping occurs, and the total number of subset sums is 6 instead of 7. Thus, the correct count is that adding a_{k+1} introduces k+1 new subset sums: the sums of size 1 to k+1 with a_{k+1} included. But in the arithmetic progression case, it's exactly k+1 new sums, leading to the total of (k+1)(k+2)/2.\n\nTherefore, by induction, if adding a_{k+1} introduces at least k+1 new subset sums, then the total number of subset sums will be at least the required bound. Since in the arithmetic progression case, exactly k+1 new sums are added (even with overlaps), and in other cases, due to the numbers being larger or arranged differently, more new sums would be added, the lower bound holds.\n\nThus, the key idea is that each new element a_{k+1} must add at least k+1 new subset sums, either by being a new singleton or by combining with previous subsets to form new sums. Even if there's an overlap in the singleton, the combinations with previous elements add k new sums (since a_{k+1} + a_i are all distinct and new), leading to at least k+1 new sums. Therefore, by induction, the total number of subset sums is at least 1 + 2 + ... +n = n(n+1)/2.\n\nYes, this seems to be the crux. When adding a_{k+1}, even if the singleton a_{k+1} duplicates an existing sum, the k sums formed by adding a_{k+1} to each of the previous k elements must be new. Because each a_i is distinct and positive, the sums a_{k+1} + a_i are all distinct. Additionally, these sums are all greater than a_{k+1} (since a_i >0), and since a_{k+1} is larger than all previous a_i (if ordered), these sums are larger than any previous sums, hence cannot overlap with existing sums except possibly the singleton a_{k+1}. However, even if a_{k+1} duplicates a previous sum, the k sums a_{k+1} + a_i are new. Therefore, the number of new subset sums added is at least k +1 (if a_{k+1} is new) or k (if a_{k+1} duplicates). But wait, if a_{k+1} duplicates a previous sum, then we only get k new sums. But the induction step