 to 39. So, when A is less than 20, the larger number is 20, so B would be 20 mod A. When A is 20 or more, since A is less than 40, the larger number is A, so B would be A mod 20. But wait, when A is 20, then both are equal, so the remainder when 20 is divided by 20 is 0. But A has to be non-zero natural numbers less than 40, so A is from 1 to 39. So, when A < 20: B = 20 mod A. When A \u2265 20: B = A mod 20.\n\nThen, we have 20 \u0398 B = 7. Since B is the result of A \u0398 20, which is either 20 mod A or A mod 20. Let's split into two cases based on whether A is less than 20 or not.\n\nCase 1: A < 20. Then, B = 20 mod A. Then, 20 \u0398 B = 7. Since B is the remainder when 20 is divided by A, which is less than A. So, B is less than A. Since A is less than 20, B is less than 20. Then, 20 \u0398 B would be the remainder when 20 is divided by B (since 20 is larger than B). So, 20 divided by B gives a remainder of 7. Therefore, 20 mod B = 7. So, in this case, we have B = 20 mod A, and 20 mod B = 7. So, we can set up the equation: 20 mod (20 mod A) = 7. Then, solve for A < 20.\n\nCase 2: A \u2265 20. Then, since A is less than 40, B = A mod 20. So, B is A - 20*k for some integer k \u2265 1, such that B is between 0 and 19. Since A is between 20 and 39, B is between 0 and 19. Then, 20 \u0398 B = 7. Since B is between 0 and 19, B is less than 20, so 20 \u0398 B is 20 mod B. But 20 mod B = 7. So, similar to case 1, but B here is A mod 20. Therefore, 20 mod (A mod 20) = 7. So, solve for A in 20 \u2264 A < 40.\n\nSo, we have two cases to consider: A < 20 and 20 \u2264 A < 40. Let's tackle them one by one.\n\nFirst, Case 1: A < 20.\n\nGiven that B = 20 mod A, and 20 mod B = 7. Let me denote B = 20 mod A. Then, 20 mod B = 7. So, 20 divided by B leaves remainder 7. So, that implies that B divides 20 - 7 = 13. Wait, no. If 20 mod B = 7, then B must be a divisor of 20 - 7 = 13, but 13 is a prime number. Wait, that's not exactly right. Let me recall that if 20 mod B = 7, then B