x + 6 \\) must be a composite number, not a prime number.\n7. Thus, the sum of four consecutive positive integers cannot be a prime number.\n\n#### Conclusion:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{No, the sum of four consecutive positive integers cannot be a prime number.}}\n\\]\n\n#### Part (b):\n1. Let's denote \\( y \\) as the smallest of the three consecutive positive integers.\n2. The three consecutive integers are then \\( y \\), \\( y+1 \\), and \\( y+2 \\).\n3. We calculate the sum of these three consecutive integers:\n   \\[\n   y + (y+1) + (y+2)\n   \\]\n4. Simplify the expression:\n   \\[\n   y + (y+1) + (y+2) = 3y + 3\n   \\]\n5. Notice that the sum \\( 3y + 3 \\) can be factored:\n   \\[\n   3y + 3 = 3(y + 1)\n   \\]\n6. Since \\( 3(y + 1) \\) is clearly a multiple of 3 and greater than 3 when \\( y \\) is a positive integer, the sum \\( 3y + 3 \\) must be a composite number if \\( y + 1 \\) is greater than 1.\n7. Thus, the sum of three consecutive positive integers cannot be a prime number.\n\n#### Conclusion:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{No, the sum of three consecutive positive integers cannot be a prime number.}}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "In the tetrahedron \\( PABC \\), if point \\( P \\)'s projection onto plane \\( ABC \\) is the circumcenter \\( O \\) of triangle \\( ABC \\), and the planes opposite to edges \\( PA, PB, \\) and \\( PC \\) have dihedral angles \\(\\alpha, \\beta,\\) and \\(\\gamma\\) respectively, and the angle between \\( PO \\) and \\( PA \\) is \\(\\varphi\\), then:\n\n(I) \\(\\cos \\angle BAC = \\frac{\\sin^2 \\frac{\\beta}{2} + \\sin^2 \\frac{\\gamma}{2} - \\sin^2 \\frac{\\alpha}{2}}{2 \\sin \\frac{\\beta}{2} \\cdot \\sin \\frac{\\gamma}{2}} \\) (and similar expressions hold for other angles);\n\n(II) \\(\\frac{\\sin \\frac{\\alpha}{2}}{\\sin \\angle BAC} = \\frac{\\sin \\frac{\\beta}{2}}{\\sin \\angle ABC} = \\frac{\\sin \\frac{\\gamma}{2}}{\\sin \\angle ACB} = \\sin \\