ace's plane is that tangent point. Therefore, the projection of the entire sphere onto the face's plane is a circle centered at the tangent point with radius equal to the sphere's radius. \n\nTherefore, for this projection to lie entirely within the face, the face must contain a circle of radius r (the sphere's radius) centered at the tangent point. That implies that the face must extend at least a distance r in every direction from the tangent point. But the face is a polygon, so all its edges must be at least a distance r away from the tangent point. \n\nBut in a polyhedron circumscribed around a sphere, each face is tangent at exactly one point. So, for each face, the tangent point is where the sphere touches it. Therefore, the projection of the sphere onto each face's plane is a circle of radius r centered at the tangent point. For this circle to lie entirely within the face, the face must not have any edges closer than r to the tangent point. \n\nSo, each large face must be a polygon where every edge is at least a distance r away from the tangent point. That seems like a significant constraint on the shape and size of the face. \n\nNow, how can we relate the number of such faces to the geometry of the polyhedron? Maybe we can use some properties of convex polyhedrons, since a polyhedron circumscribed around a sphere must be convex (the sphere has to touch each face without crossing any edges). \n\nLet me recall that in a convex polyhedron, the tangent planes at the points of contact with the sphere can be related to the dual polyhedron. But I'm not sure if that's helpful here. \n\nAlternatively, maybe we can use the concept of the normal vectors to the faces. Each face has a normal vector pointing from the center of the sphere to the tangent point. If the sphere is centered at the origin, then the normal vector to each face is in the direction of the tangent point. \n\nIf we have multiple faces, each with their normal vectors. The condition that the projection 