 =729\n\nNow, with L=27, we can find coordinates of B1 and B2.\n\nRecall that coordinates of B1:\n\n( (L -9)a +162 ) / L, ( (L -9)b ) / L ) = ( (27 -9)a +162 ) /27, ( (27 -9)b ) /27 ) = (18a +162)/27, 18b/27 ) = ( (18(a +9))/27, (18b)/27 ) = ( (2(a +9))/3, (2b)/3 )\n\nSimilarly, coordinates of B2:\n\n(6a +18*(27 -6 )) /27, 6b/27 ) = (6a +18*21)/27, (6b)/27 ) = (6a +378)/27, 2b/9 ) = (2a +126)/9, 2b/9 )\n\nTherefore, B1 is at ( (2(a +9))/3, (2b)/3 ) and B2 is at ( (2a +126)/9, (2b)/9 )\n\nNow, we need to compute B1B2. The distance between B1 and B2.\n\nCompute the difference in x-coordinates:\n\n(2a +126)/9 - (2(a +9))/3 = [2a +126 -6(a +9)] /9 = [2a +126 -6a -54]/9 = [ -4a +72 ] /9 = (-4a +72)/9\n\nDifference in y-coordinates:\n\n(2b)/9 - (2b)/3 = (2b -6b)/9 = (-4b)/9\n\nTherefore, distance B1B2:\n\nsqrt[ ( (-4a +72)/9 )^2 + ( (-4b)/9 )^2 ] = (1/9)sqrt[ ( -4a +72 )^2 + ( -4b )^2 ] = (1/9)sqrt[ 16(a -18)^2 + 16b^2 ] = (4/9)sqrt[ (a -18)^2 + b^2 ]\n\nBut from equation (4), (18 -a)^2 + b^2=729, so sqrt[ (a -18)^2 + b^2 ]=27.\n\nTherefore, B1B2= (4/9)*27= (4/9)*27=12\n\nSo, the length of B1B2 is 12 units.\n\n**Final Answer**\n\\boxed{12}", "deepseek_solution": "Given triangle \\(ABC\\) with points \\(A_1\\) and \\(A_2\\) on side \\(BC\\) such that \\(BA_1 = 6\\), \\(A_1A_2 = 8\\), and \\(CA_2 = 4\\). Points \\(B_1\\) and \\(B_2\\) are on side \\(AC\\) such that \\(AB_1 = 9\\) and \\(CB_2 = 6\\). Segments \\(AA_1\\) and \\(BB_1\\) intersect at \\(K\\), and segments \\(AA_2\\) and \\(BB_2\\) intersect at \\(L\\). Points \\(K\\), \\(L\\), and \\(C\\) are collinear. We need to find the length of \\(B_1B_2\\).\n\n1. **Coordinates and Parameters**:\n   - Place \\(B\\) at \\((0, 0)\\), \\(C\\) at \\((18, 0)\\), \\(A_1\\) at \\((6, 0)\\), and \\(A_2\\) at \\((14, 0)\\).\n   - Let \\(A\\) be \\((a, b)\\). The length of \\(AC\\) is \\(L = \\sqrt{(18 - a)^2 + b^2}\\).\n\n2. **Coordinates of \\(B_1\\) and \\(B_2\\)**:\n   - \\(B_1\\) divides \\(AC\\) in the ratio \\(9 : (L - 9)\\),